From 83ea0c9c9d12fafd34d27ad86cc9843fa55ecae4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 23:03:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#119, #120): draw mono from the scratch we own

THE DIAGNOSIS, settled on hardware and not re-derived here. The browser#99
recovery draw fires and yields n=0 in normal operation. ProduceSkia SUCCEEDS
(produce=0); the refusal is D3DImageBacking::ValidateBeginAccess -- without
SHARED_IMAGE_USAGE_CONCURRENT_READ_WRITE, an OPEN WRITE ACCESS refuses every
access including a read, and a canvas that is producing holds write across its
whole frame. %TEMP%\dxr_fs.log: ~830 "Already being accessed for write" each
paired 1:1 with "Unable to initialize graphite::BackendTextures", interleaved
with the n=0 recovery lines, zero keyed-mutex and zero fence failures.

NOT the usage bit. Adding CONCURRENT_READ_WRITE at drawing_buffer.cc:2065 would
licence sampling a canvas MID-WRITE: the recovery would paint a torn canvas and
the weave leg -- which takes the same read access -- would weave a TORN SBS,
strictly worse than 0074's keep-previous. It is also a Blink change carried
through every rebase to fix a compositor-side SOURCING decision, and today's
DISPLAY_READ / RASTER_READ consumers depend on the exclusion it removes.

INSTEAD: recover from the per-target scratch we already own.

1. THE PRIMITIVE. attempt_submit already composes each tile's finished weave
   input into a per-target scratch SharedImage (created at the DisplayXRScratch
   site, DISPLAY_READ|DISPLAY_WRITE|CPU_READ, sized to the tile's clipped rect;
   filled with the clean canvas SBS through the quad's UV window over the 0050
   pre-blended page backdrop). We are its ONLY writer, so it can never be
   write-contended. The recovery now draws the LEFT HALF of where the SBS landed
   in that scratch, magnified 2x horizontally back over the tile -- the mono
   twin of 0074's BatchKeepPrevious, with the same licence and the same
   scratch_size == rect.size() guard. The canvas-resource leg stays as the
   fallback for a tile the weave has never composed.

2. THE PREDICATE. recovery_source_actually_available(tile) is answered from
   bookkeeping the compose recorded -- scratch_has_clean_sbs (set ONLY from
   src_from_canvas, never from an |output| snapshot, which on a suppressed frame
   IS the hole), the canvas mailbox it composed from, where the SBS landed, and
   at which rect. No probe of a contended resource, no per-frame cost. Both
   platforms' failures were downstream of a non-zero mailbox, so a cheap
   existence check passes and still leaves a void; this one cannot. Placement is
   guarded: the scratch survives a MOVE (only a size change reallocates it), so
   only two placements are admitted -- the exact one, or a pure translation of
   the trivial full-scratch layout to the live rect. A clipped tile that also
   moved declines and falls through to today's behaviour rather than ghosting.

3. #120 -- MONO UNDER FROST, and the ordering IS the bug. Inside a frosted rect
   the region is drawn by nobody: the canvas quad was withheld from the page
   raster and D' (0068) clips the woven draw-back out of backdrop-filter rects.
   The frost then blurs a dark void. Skia samples a backdrop filter from
   whatever is already on the canvas at saveLayer time, so the fix cannot live
   in the GPU-side hook -- by then the glass is composited and mono over it
   would destroy the crispness D' exists to protect. ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit
   therefore records each frosted rect against the TILE'S OWN CANVAS QUAD;
   ShouldSkipQuad stops skipping that quad; and SkiaRenderer draws it MONO,
   clipped into exactly those rects, at its own place in the page raster. The
   order is structural, not hopeful: a frosted rect is only ever recorded for a
   quad at front-to-back index i < k(T), and DrawRenderPassOnGpu walks
   BackToFront, so index k is painted before index i, always. D's contract is
   untouched -- the glass still samples pre-weave page pixels and stays crisp,
   the depth behind it still reads flat -- but flat is now literally true.

NOT IN THIS PATCH: the over-plane decoupling. An earlier draft of this change
also carried a second, independent fix -- hoisting the Phase-2 over-plane
composite out from behind the `batch && !wove_this_frame` gate so it runs on all
five of phase (B)'s exits rather than only on woven frames. It has been SPLIT
OUT and deferred: the Android arm's parallel investigation shipped a
device-verified fix for the same function on its own `#if` arm, and two
competing implementations of one function is the thing to avoid. The Windows
generalization lands as its own follow-up; the split-out hunks are preserved
verbatim at docs/pending/overplane-generalization.patch.txt.

That split is clean by construction -- the two halves share no line and no
symbol. Every hunk this patch keeps precedes every hunk that was removed, and
the removed lambda's state (over_rep / over_access / over_plane_drawn) is
declared and consumed entirely within the removed hunks. What this patch keeps
is the mono primitive only: the scratch-sourced recovery source, its predicate,
and the renderer-side #120 mono-under-frost ordering fix.

The ORDERING claim above still holds and is the reason the two were drafted
together: on a withheld frame the region wants mono first and the over-plane on
top. With this patch alone the mono goes down; whether the over-plane is
composited over it on a weave-less frame is exactly what the deferred follow-up
decides.

Patch 0081's withhold semantics are untouched: this changes only what is DRAWN
in a withheld region, never what is submitted. #131's topmost-wins consumes this
primitive and lands separately.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
